# twisted.obj — cube.obj with ONE face wound the wrong way round.
#
# Lesson 3.4 left a debt: back-face culling discards triangles by winding, and
# nothing in the engine had ever checked that a mesh's winding was consistent,
# because every mesh so far was one we typed. This file is what an inconsistently
# wound export actually looks like — and it is indistinguishable from cube.obj
# until you either cull back faces (the top of the cube vanishes and you see the
# inside) or run validate(), which reports the two directed edges that disagree.
#
# The only change from cube.obj is the fifth face, whose four corners are listed
# in reverse. Its `vn` still claims to point at +y, which is exactly how this
# survives in the wild: the normals look right, so the file looks right.
#
# Authored for "Build a Professional 3D Game Engine". MIT.

o cube_twisted

v -0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v  0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v  0.5  0.5 -0.5
v -0.5  0.5 -0.5
v -0.5 -0.5  0.5
v  0.5 -0.5  0.5
v  0.5  0.5  0.5
v -0.5  0.5  0.5

vt 0.0 0.0
vt 1.0 0.0
vt 1.0 1.0
vt 0.0 1.0

vn  0.0  0.0 -1.0
vn  0.0  0.0  1.0
vn -1.0  0.0  0.0
vn  1.0  0.0  0.0
vn  0.0  1.0  0.0
vn  0.0 -1.0  0.0

f 1/1/1 4/4/1 3/3/1 2/2/1
f 5/1/2 6/2/2 7/3/2 8/4/2
f 1/1/3 5/2/3 8/3/3 4/4/3
f 2/1/4 3/4/4 7/3/4 6/2/4
f 3/4/5 7/3/5 8/2/5 4/1/5
f 1/1/6 2/2/6 6/3/6 5/4/6
